\vspace{0.2cm}
\textcolor{primaryblue}{\textbf{Solutions:}}

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Graph $G_1$} ($n=5$ vertices, 8 edges):

Degrees: $\deg(a)=4,\;\deg(b)=3,\;\deg(c)=3,\;\deg(d)=2,\;\deg(e)=4$.

Try circuit: $a\to b\to c\to e\to d\to a$.

Edges: $\{a,b\}✓,\;\{b,c\}✓,\;\{c,e\}✓,\;
\{e,d\}✓,\;\{d,a\}✓$.

All 5 vertices visited once, returned to $a$.

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{$G_1$ has a Hamiltonian circuit:}}
$a\to b\to c\to e\to d\to a$. \checkmark

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Graph $G_2$} ($n=4$ vertices, 5 edges):

Try circuit: $a\to b\to c\to d\to a$.

Edges: $\{a,b\}✓,\;\{b,c\}✓,\;\{c,d\}✓,\;
\{d,a\}✓$.

All 4 vertices visited once, returned to $a$.
(Diagonal edge $a$--$c$ is simply skipped --- allowed in Hamiltonian.)

\textcolor{darkgreen}{\textbf{$G_2$ has a Hamiltonian circuit:}}
$a\to b\to c\to d\to a$. \checkmark

\vspace{0.3cm}
\textbf{Graph $G_3$} ($n=7$ vertices, 7 edges):

Check degrees: $\deg(a)=1,\;\deg(b)=2,\;\deg(c)=3,\;\deg(d)=2,\;
\deg(e)=2,\;\deg(f)=3,\;\deg(g)=1$.

Vertices $a$ and $g$ each have degree 1 (pendant vertices).
A Hamiltonian circuit would require every vertex to have
degree $\geq 2$ in the circuit --- pendant vertices can only be
endpoints, not circuit vertices.

\textcolor{red}{\textbf{$G_3$ has no Hamiltonian circuit.}}

Try Hamiltonian path: $a\to b\to c\to d\to e\to f\to g$.

Check edges: $\{a,b\}✓,\;\{b,c\}✓,\;
\{c,d\}✓,\;\{d,e\}✓,\;\{e,f\}✓,\;
\{f,g\}✓$.

All 7 vertices visited exactly once. (Edge $\{c,f\}$ is skipped.)

\textcolor{accentorange}{\textbf{$G_3$ has a Hamiltonian path:}}
$a\to b\to c\to d\to e\to f\to g$. ✓

